# 17. Architectural Integrity & Minimal-Fix Governance

## Problem Statement

AI coding agents frequently introduce unnecessary “safe fixes” that solve symptoms instead of root causes.

Common issues include:

* Overengineering
* Unnecessary abstraction layers
* Wrapper proliferation
* Duplicate utility creation
* Architecture drift
* Hidden side effects
* SOLID violations
* YAGNI violations
* KISS violations
* Large unrelated refactors
* “Fix-all” modifications
* Defensive code expansion

These behaviors increase:

* maintenance cost
* debugging complexity
* token consumption
* regression risk
* architectural inconsistency

---

# 18. Root Cause First Policy

AI agents must prioritize:

1. Root cause identification
2. Minimal targeted fix
3. Existing architecture reuse
4. Smallest possible code delta

Rule:

> Never redesign architecture to fix a localized issue.

---

# 19. Minimal Change Principle

AI-generated fixes must:

* modify the fewest files possible
* preserve existing architecture
* avoid unrelated refactors
* avoid creating new abstractions unless required
* avoid introducing new services/classes/hooks/helpers without justification

Preferred:

* targeted fixes
* localized changes
* architecture-preserving updates

---

# 20. SOLID / KISS / YAGNI Enforcement

Every AI-generated change must pass:

## SOLID

* Single Responsibility
* Open/Closed
* Liskov Substitution
* Interface Segregation
* Dependency Inversion

## KISS

* Keep implementation simple
* Avoid unnecessary patterns

## YAGNI

* Do not build speculative solutions
* Do not add future-proof abstractions without actual need

Rule:

> Future hypothetical scalability is NOT justification for architectural expansion.

---

# 21. No Hidden Refactor Rule

AI agents must NOT:

* rename unrelated files
* move folders unnecessarily
* rewrite stable modules
* change architecture patterns
* alter naming conventions
* introduce framework migrations

Unless explicitly requested.

---

# 22. Existing Pattern Reuse Policy

Before creating:

* utility
* hook
* service
* helper
* component
* abstraction

AI must first:

1. search existing codebase
2. reuse established patterns
3. extend existing modules if appropriate

Rule:

> Duplication caused by AI-generated parallel abstractions is prohibited.

---

# 23. Error Resolution Workflow

Before implementing any fix:

## Step 1 — Reproduce

* understand actual error
* isolate root cause

## Step 2 — Analyze Scope

* determine affected boundaries
* avoid broad impact

## Step 3 — Check Existing Architecture

* identify reusable patterns
* avoid duplicate logic

## Step 4 — Apply Minimal Fix

* smallest viable correction

## Step 5 — Validate

* ensure no regression
* verify architecture consistency

---

# 24. Forbidden AI Behaviors

AI agents are prohibited from:

* “fixing everything”
* speculative refactoring
* introducing unnecessary generic abstractions
* rewriting stable modules
* creating duplicate services/hooks/helpers
* adding fallback chains without reason
* adding defensive complexity for low-risk scenarios
* implementing architecture redesign for local bugs

---

# 25. Human Approval Gates

Human review required before:

* folder restructuring
* dependency changes
* framework upgrades
* architecture changes
* abstraction introduction
* service extraction
* large-scale refactors
* state management rewrites

---

# 26. Architecture Preservation Principle

The existing architecture is the default source of truth.

AI agents must:

* preserve conventions
* preserve boundaries
* preserve module ownership
* preserve dependency flow
* preserve coding standards

Rule:

> Consistency is more valuable than theoretical perfection.

---

# 27. Preferred Engineering Philosophy

Preferred:

* pragmatic engineering
* maintainable code
* readable code
* small targeted fixes
* iterative improvements

Avoid:

* academic architecture
* abstraction obsession
* enterprise overengineering
* speculative scalability

---

# 28. AI Agent Success Criteria

A successful AI-generated fix:

✓ fixes root cause
✓ minimizes code changes
✓ preserves architecture
✓ follows existing patterns
✓ avoids regressions
✓ maintains SOLID/KISS/YAGNI
✓ avoids unnecessary abstractions
✓ remains understandable by humans

---

# 29. Core Governance Principle

AI agents are assistants to engineering judgment — not autonomous architects.

Rule:

> The best fix is usually the smallest correct fix.
